# CLAUDE.md — Project Workflow ({{PROJECT_NAME}})

This file establishes a persistent, interruption-safe development workflow.

> If a Claude session stops unexpectedly at any moment, another Claude session must be able to
> enter this repository and continue the work without needing the previous conversation.
>
> The repository and Git history are the source of truth when Git is available. When Git is not
> available, the `.claude/` state files are the source of truth. Do not depend on conversation
> memory in either case.

---

## Browser Project / Git Availability

This project may run inside a browser-based Claude.ai chat (files provided via a connected GitHub
repository or pasted directly) rather than a local Claude Code terminal. In that environment there
may be no local `.git` directory by default.

**Do not assume Git CLI is available or unavailable — check.** At the start of a session:
```bash
which git && git --version
```

### Confirmed working method: committing/pushing from a Claude.ai browser session

Claude.ai chat sessions with the code-execution/bash tool enabled run inside a sandboxed container
that typically has `git` installed and network egress allowed to `github.com` / `api.github.com` /
`codeload.github.com`. This means Claude can clone this repo, make changes, commit, and push —
even though the browser chat interface itself has no persistent local checkout and no stored
GitHub credential.

Procedure, once per chat session that needs to push:

1. Verify: `which git`, `curl -sI https://api.github.com` (a 403 is fine — confirms reachability).
2. Ask the user for a GitHub token **once**, at the point a push is first needed. Recommend a
   fine-grained Personal Access Token scoped to only this repo, `Contents: Read & Write`, short
   expiry.
3. Clone: `git clone https://<TOKEN>@github.com/<owner>/<repo>.git`
4. Do the work inside that clone.
5. Commit with a `feat(TASK-xxx): ...` / `checkpoint(TASK-xxx): ...` / `fix(BUG-xxx): ...` message.
6. Push, masking the token in any output shown to the user:
   `git push origin <branch> 2>&1 | sed "s/<TOKEN>/***TOKEN***/g"`
   Only report success if the actual `<old-sha>..<new-sha> branch -> branch` line appears.
7. Reuse the same token for the rest of that chat session — don't re-ask per commit. Do ask again
   in a new chat session.
8. Afterward, strip the token from the local remote (`git remote set-url origin
   https://github.com/<owner>/<repo>.git`) and tell the user once that the pasted token is now in
   the chat transcript and should be treated as no longer secret — recommend revoking/rotating it.

This does not contradict "never persist a token" below — it never gets written to a tracked file,
a commit, or `.claude/*`; it only exists transiently in that session's sandbox process/environment.

**If Git CLI or the sandbox is unavailable:**
- Do not claim the project files are missing — they may still be available via a Claude.ai Project
  synced from GitHub (read-only) or pasted directly.
- Do not fabricate Git status, commit SHAs, branch names, or push results.
- Treat `.claude/*` as the persistent, authoritative session state instead of Git history.
- State plainly that Git operations cannot be performed in this environment.

