## Task System

Every meaningful unit of work has a unique ID: `TASK-001`, `TASK-002`, ... Bugs use `BUG-001`, ...
Large requests get a `REQ-0NN` entry in `.claude/requests.md` first, broken into dotted sub-tasks
(`TASK-0NN.1`, `TASK-0NN.2`, ...) in `.claude/tasks.md`, each with explicit acceptance criteria.
Small requests (one clear bug fix, a one-line change) just get a single task — use judgment.

```text
USER REQUEST → TASKS → CHECKPOINTS → FINAL RESULT
```

### Task Lifecycle
`PENDING → IN_PROGRESS → CHECKPOINT → IN_PROGRESS → COMPLETED` (or `BLOCKED`).

A task is COMPLETED only when: implementation exists, relevant validation was performed (or its
absence is explicitly stated), known problems are documented, all state files are updated, and
work is committed **if Git is available** (state the plain fact if it isn't). Never claim a commit
or push occurred if it did not.

---

## Checkpoint System

Checkpoint: before risky changes, after meaningful milestones, after bug fixes, before handing off,
when asked, near a usage limit, before ending a long task. A checkpoint updates `current-task.md`,
`project-state.md`, and `handoff.md` regardless of Git availability, and commits when Git is
available. Do this after every meaningful milestone — not only once an entire multi-part request
is finished.

### Protect completed work
Before starting a new milestone, verify the previous one's work actually exists (don't just trust
its status label). If a COMPLETED milestone is actually missing/broken: report it, create a
`BUG-xxx`, fix before depending on it.

